WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:02.760
all right, let's get into audience questions
for the week.

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:03.760
Here's the first one.

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:04.760
Hey David.

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:08.490
Why do you keep saying your history and it
seems like obvious BS.

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:10.240
Please stop saying that.

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:13.480
So, so this is a, this is an ongoing thing.

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:18.290
According to the United States census, as
an immigrant from Argentina who came to the

00:00:18.290 --> 00:00:24.230
United States and learned English when I got
here, the us census Bureau says that I am

00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:26.370
indeed Hispanic or Latino.

00:00:26.370 --> 00:00:34.570
Hispanic or Latino is a person of Cuban, Mexican,
Puerto Rican, central or South American descent

00:00:34.570 --> 00:00:40.020
or other Spanish culture or origin regardless
of race.

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:47.899
Okay, so a Hispanic or Latino person can be
of any race of any ancestry, any ethnicity

00:00:47.899 --> 00:00:52.149
that speaks Spanish or Portuguese from these
countries that are listed.

00:00:52.149 --> 00:00:53.149
That is me.

00:00:53.149 --> 00:00:54.149
Okay.

00:00:54.149 --> 00:00:59.870
Now there's no question that because my ethnicity
is Ashkenazi Jewish and I moved to the United

00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:04.519
States young enough to be speaking English
mostly without an accent, except for some

00:01:04.519 --> 00:01:08.039
random words that some in the audience or
acquainted with.

00:01:08.039 --> 00:01:14.570
I am not ignorant to the reality that my experience
as an adult in the United States once I learned

00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:19.849
English in the United States is effectively
that of a white American with whatever degree

00:01:19.849 --> 00:01:21.659
of privilege that affords me.

00:01:21.659 --> 00:01:25.549
And whatever your belief is about white privilege,
that's not what the argument is about.

00:01:25.549 --> 00:01:27.280
Whatever degree you believe exists.

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:31.040
Um, and it would be a little weird to think
that there is no degree of white privilege.

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:35.689
I understand that I have that it is confirmed
to me by virtue of having come to the United

00:01:35.689 --> 00:01:38.959
States early enough to, to speak English without
an accent.

00:01:38.959 --> 00:01:43.780
And because of the way I look, I'm not identified
as Hispanic, but as far as the census is concerned,

00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:51.039
uh, I am Hispanic and of course I was raised
in Hispanic culture and I still of course

00:01:51.039 --> 00:01:57.200
have family in Argentina and spend time there
and identify a with all of the things that

00:01:57.200 --> 00:01:58.679
are implicit in that.

00:01:58.679 --> 00:02:04.029
So, you know, when I talk about on the show
in the context of it, the immigration debate

00:02:04.029 --> 00:02:10.970
or whatever, and I talk about being Hispanic,
it's sort of tongue in cheek in the sense

00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:17.019
that, I mean, listen, my, my view is primarily
informed by being, uh, you, you, you know,

00:02:17.019 --> 00:02:25.480
and an educated person in the United States
who has empathy and cares about economics

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:29.209
and national security and treating people
with dignity.

00:02:29.209 --> 00:02:35.849
And the reality is that according to the census,
being Hispanic is not the primary lens through

00:02:35.849 --> 00:02:39.610
which I am seeing these issues, at least not
in a conscious way.

00:02:39.610 --> 00:02:42.190
Uh, but, uh, it's not BS.

00:02:42.190 --> 00:02:47.579
And, um, the, it's funny, there's people in
the audience who think that the idea of me

00:02:47.579 --> 00:02:51.019
being Hispanic is BS and there's a contingent
of hatred.

00:02:51.019 --> 00:02:58.379
It's in the audience that regularly attack
me in anti Hispanic ways with the slurs that

00:02:58.379 --> 00:03:04.269
you often hear about, uh, central and South
Americans and with go back to, you know, Argentina

00:03:04.269 --> 00:03:05.269
type of stuff.

00:03:05.269 --> 00:03:06.269
In any case.

00:03:06.269 --> 00:03:08.099
Yeah, I am Hispanic according to the census.

00:03:08.099 --> 00:03:16.330
Um, my, my culture is a, you know, a mix of
South American, Argentinian and uh, uh, cultural

00:03:16.330 --> 00:03:21.109
Judaism and just growing up in the Northeast
and, and you know, interestingly enough, a

00:03:21.109 --> 00:03:28.310
lot of my culture is more a metropolitan city
culture than anything else, which is often

00:03:28.310 --> 00:03:34.760
much more similar between countries than the
culture often is between rural and urban areas

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:36.310
within the United States.

00:03:36.310 --> 00:03:38.950
But that's probably a conversation, uh, for
a different day.

